/-!
# Integral Cayley graphs on normal sets of involutions

A finite graph is integral if all eigenvalues of its adjacency matrix are integers. This file
proves the answer "yes" to part (a) of Kourovka Notebook Problem 19.50 (D. V. Lytkina): if a
finite group `G` is generated by a set `R` of involutions closed under conjugation, the Cayley
graph `Cay(G, R)` is integral (`involutionCayleyGraphIntegral_holds`). The generation hypothesis
is not needed: `hasIntegralSpectrum_adjMatrix_mulCayley_of_involutions` proves integrality for
every set of involutions closed under conjugation.

`Cay(G, R)` is Mathlib's `SimpleGraph.mulCayley R`: vertex set `G`, `x ≠ y` adjacent when
`x⁻¹ y ∈ R` or `y⁻¹ x ∈ R`; here `R = R⁻¹` and `1 ∉ R`, so `x` and `y` are adjacent exactly when
`x⁻¹ y ∈ R`. Integral means every complex root of the characteristic polynomial of the integer
adjacency matrix (`SimpleGraph.adjMatrix`) is an integer (`HasIntegralSpectrum`).

## Proof

The complex adjacency operator `A` of `Cay(G, R)` is the sum of the right translations
`T_r : v ↦ (x ↦ v (x r))` by the elements `r ∈ R`. Since `R` is closed under conjugation, `A`
commutes with every `T_g`, so each eigenspace `E` of `A`, say for the eigenvalue `μ`, is invariant
under every `T_r`. Taking traces on `E` gives `μ · dim E = ∑_{r ∈ R} tr(T_r|_E)`. Each `T_r|_E` is
an involution, so its eigenvalues are `±1` and its trace is an integer. Hence `μ` is rational, and
as a root of the monic integer characteristic polynomial it is an integer. This is the Kourovka
Archive's character-theoretic argument, `μ = (1 / ψ(1)) ∑_{r ∈ R} ψ(r)`, applied to the character
`ψ` of the representation of `G` on `E` rather than to an irreducible character, which avoids
Schur's lemma.

Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 19.50 (D. V.
Lytkina). UnsolvedMath record UM-00701598 (`KOU-19.50`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.
-/

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.IntegralCayleyGraphsKourovka19Problem50Wanted

/-- A square integer matrix is integral: every complex root of its characteristic polynomial (every
eigenvalue) is an integer. -/
def HasIntegralSpectrum {n : Type*} [Fintype n] [DecidableEq n] (A : Matrix n n ℤ) : Prop :=
  ∀ μ : ℂ, (A.map (Int.castRingHom ℂ)).charpoly.IsRoot μ → ∃ k : ℤ, (k : ℂ) = μ

open Classical in
/-- **The Kourovka 19.50(a) integrality claim.** This is the proposition that for every finite
group $G$ and every subset $R \subseteq G$ such that every element of $R$ is an involution
($r^2 = 1$ and $r \ne 1$), $R$ is closed under conjugation ($g r g^{-1} \in R$ for all $r \in R$
and $g \in G$) and $R$ generates $G$, the Cayley graph $\mathrm{Cay}(G, R)$ is integral. Here
$\mathrm{Cay}(G, R)$ has vertex set $G$, distinct $x$ and $y$ being adjacent when $x^{-1} y \in R$
or $y^{-1} x \in R$, and a graph is integral when every eigenvalue of its integer adjacency matrix
(every complex root of the characteristic polynomial of that matrix) is an integer. -/
def involutionCayleyGraphIntegral : Prop :=
  ∀ (G : Type) [Group G] [Fintype G] [DecidableEq G] (R : Set G),
    (∀ r ∈ R, r ^ 2 = 1 ∧ r ≠ 1) → (∀ r ∈ R, ∀ g : G, g * r * g⁻¹ ∈ R) →
    Subgroup.closure R = ⊤ → HasIntegralSpectrum ((SimpleGraph.mulCayley R).adjMatrix ℤ)

/-- The trace of an involution of a finite-dimensional complex vector space is an integer: its
eigenvalues are `1` and `-1`. -/
private theorem exists_intCast_eq_trace_of_mul_self_eq_one {V : Type*} [AddCommGroup V]
    [Module ℂ V] [FiniteDimensional ℂ V] (u : Module.End ℂ V) (hu : u * u = 1) :
    ∃ k : ℤ, (k : ℂ) = LinearMap.trace ℂ V u := by
  rw [Module.End.trace_eq_sum_roots_charpoly_of_splits (IsAlgClosed.splits _)]
  have hroots : ∀ x ∈ u.charpoly.roots, x ∈ (Int.castRingHom ℂ).range := by
    intro x hx
    obtain ⟨v, hv⟩ := ((Module.End.hasEigenvalue_iff_isRoot_charpoly u x).2
      (Polynomial.isRoot_of_mem_roots hx)).exists_hasEigenvector
    have hx2 : x * x = 1 := by
      have h1 : (u * u) v = (x * x) • v := by
        rw [mul_apply_eq_comp, hv.apply_eq_smul, map_smul, hv.apply_eq_smul, smul_smul]
      rw [hu, one_apply_eq_self] at h1
      exact smul_left_injective ℂ hv.2 (by simpa using h1.symm)
    rcases mul_self_eq_one_iff.mp hx2 with rfl | rfl
    · exact ⟨1, by simp⟩
    · exact ⟨-1, by simp⟩
  obtain ⟨k, hk⟩ := (Int.castRingHom ℂ).range.multiset_sum_mem _ hroots
  exact ⟨k, by simpa using hk⟩

section RightTranslation

variable {G : Type*} [Group G]

/-- Right translation by `g` on complex functions on `G`: `v ↦ (x ↦ v (x * g))`. -/
private noncomputable def rightTransl (g : G) : Module.End ℂ (G → ℂ) :=
  LinearMap.funLeft ℂ ℂ (· * g)

private theorem rightTransl_apply (g : G) (v : G → ℂ) (x : G) :
    rightTransl g v x = v (x * g) :=
  rfl

/-- Right translation is multiplicative: `T_g T_h = T_{g h}`. -/
private theorem rightTransl_mul (g h : G) :
    rightTransl g * rightTransl h = rightTransl (g * h) :=
  LinearMap.ext fun v => funext fun x => by
    simp only [mul_apply_eq_comp, rightTransl_apply, mul_assoc]

/-- Right translation by `1` is the identity. -/
private theorem rightTransl_one : rightTransl (1 : G) = 1 :=
  LinearMap.ext fun v => funext fun x => by
    simp only [rightTransl_apply, mul_one, one_apply_eq_self]

/-- In `Cay(G, R)` for a set `R` of involutions, `x` and `x * g` are adjacent exactly when
`g ∈ R`. -/
private theorem mulCayley_adj_mul_right_iff {R : Set G} (hinv : ∀ r ∈ R, r ^ 2 = 1 ∧ r ≠ 1)
    (x g : G) : (SimpleGraph.mulCayley R).Adj x (x * g) ↔ g ∈ R := by
  have hR : ∀ r ∈ R, r⁻¹ = r := fun r hr =>
    inv_eq_of_mul_eq_one_right (by rw [← sq]; exact (hinv r hr).1)
  rw [SimpleGraph.mulCayley_adj, inv_mul_cancel_left, mul_inv_rev, inv_mul_cancel_right, ne_eq,
    left_eq_mul]
  refine ⟨fun ⟨_, h⟩ => h.elim id fun h => ?_, fun h => ⟨(hinv g h).2, Or.inl h⟩⟩
  rwa [← inv_inv g, hR _ h]

/-- For a set `R` of involutions, the complex adjacency operator of `Cay(G, R)` is the sum of
the right translations by the elements of `R`. -/
private theorem toLin'_adjMatrix_eq_sum [Fintype G] [DecidableEq G] {R : Set G}
    [DecidableRel (SimpleGraph.mulCayley R).Adj] [DecidablePred (· ∈ R)]
    (hinv : ∀ r ∈ R, r ^ 2 = 1 ∧ r ≠ 1) :
    Matrix.toLin' (((SimpleGraph.mulCayley R).adjMatrix ℤ).map (Int.castRingHom ℂ)) =
      ∑ g ∈ Finset.univ.filter (· ∈ R), rightTransl g := by
  refine LinearMap.ext fun v => funext fun x => ?_
  rw [Matrix.toLin'_apply, LinearMap.sum_apply, Finset.sum_apply, Finset.sum_filter]
  simp only [rightTransl_apply, Matrix.mulVec, dotProduct, Matrix.map_apply,
    SimpleGraph.adjMatrix_apply]
  rw [← Equiv.sum_comp (Equiv.mulLeft x)]
  refine Finset.sum_congr rfl fun g _ => ?_
  simp only [Equiv.coe_mulLeft, mulCayley_adj_mul_right_iff hinv]
  split_ifs <;> simp

end RightTranslation

/-- **Cayley graphs on normal sets of involutions are integral.** Let `R` be a set of involutions
of a finite group `G` that is closed under conjugation. Then every eigenvalue of the Cayley graph
`Cay(G, R)` is an integer. Unlike `involutionCayleyGraphIntegral_holds`, this does not assume that
`R` generates `G`, and it holds for every decidability instance of the adjacency relation. -/
theorem hasIntegralSpectrum_adjMatrix_mulCayley_of_involutions {G : Type*} [Group G] [Fintype G]
    [DecidableEq G] (R : Set G) [DecidableRel (SimpleGraph.mulCayley R).Adj]
    (hinv : ∀ r ∈ R, r ^ 2 = 1 ∧ r ≠ 1) (hconj : ∀ r ∈ R, ∀ g : G, g * r * g⁻¹ ∈ R) :
    HasIntegralSpectrum ((SimpleGraph.mulCayley R).adjMatrix ℤ) := by
  classical
  intro μ hμ
  set B := (SimpleGraph.mulCayley R).adjMatrix ℤ
  set S := Finset.univ.filter (· ∈ R)
  set f : Module.End ℂ (G → ℂ) := Matrix.toLin' (B.map (Int.castRingHom ℂ))
  have hf : f = ∑ g ∈ S, rightTransl g := toLin'_adjMatrix_eq_sum hinv
  have hmemS : ∀ g, g ∈ S ↔ g ∈ R := fun g => by simp [S]
  -- `f` commutes with every right translation, because `R` is closed under conjugation.
  have hcomm : ∀ h : G, Commute f (rightTransl h) := by
    intro h
    rw [hf, Commute, SemiconjBy, Finset.sum_mul, Finset.mul_sum]
    simp only [rightTransl_mul]
    refine Finset.sum_nbij' (fun g => h⁻¹ * g * h) (fun g => h * g * h⁻¹) ?_ ?_ ?_ ?_ ?_
    · intro g hg
      rw [hmemS] at hg ⊢
      simpa using hconj g hg h⁻¹
    · intro g hg
      rw [hmemS] at hg ⊢
      exact hconj g hg h
    · intro g _
      group
    · intro g _
      group
    · intro g _
      congr 1
      group
  -- `μ` is an eigenvalue of `f`; let `E` be its eigenspace.
  have hμf : Module.End.HasEigenvalue f μ := by
    rw [Module.End.hasEigenvalue_iff_isRoot_charpoly, Matrix.charpoly_toLin']
    exact hμ
  set E := Module.End.eigenspace f μ
  have hmaps : ∀ g : G, ∀ v ∈ E, rightTransl g v ∈ E := fun g =>
    Module.End.mapsTo_genEigenspace_of_comm (hcomm g) μ 1
  -- On `E`, the right translations by the elements of `R` sum to `μ • 1`.
  have hsum : ∑ g ∈ S, (rightTransl g).restrict (hmaps g) = μ • (1 : Module.End ℂ E) := by
    refine LinearMap.ext fun v => Subtype.ext ?_
    simp only [LinearMap.coe_sum, Finset.sum_apply, Submodule.coe_sum,
      LinearMap.coe_restrict_apply, LinearMap.smul_apply, one_apply_eq_self, Submodule.coe_smul]
    rw [← LinearMap.sum_apply, ← hf]
    exact Module.End.mem_eigenspace_iff.mp v.2
  -- Each of them is an involution of `E`, so its trace is an integer.
  have hint : ∀ g ∈ S, ∃ k : ℤ, (k : ℂ) =
      LinearMap.trace ℂ E ((rightTransl g).restrict (hmaps g)) := by
    intro g hg
    refine exists_intCast_eq_trace_of_mul_self_eq_one _ ?_
    have hgg : g * g = 1 := by rw [← sq]; exact (hinv g ((hmemS g).1 hg)).1
    refine LinearMap.ext fun v => Subtype.ext ?_
    simp only [mul_apply_eq_comp, LinearMap.coe_restrict_apply, one_apply_eq_self]
    rw [← mul_apply_eq_comp, rightTransl_mul, hgg, rightTransl_one, one_apply_eq_self]
  -- Taking traces, `μ * dim E` is an integer `m`.
  have htr : μ * (Module.finrank ℂ E : ℂ) =
      ∑ g ∈ S, LinearMap.trace ℂ E ((rightTransl g).restrict (hmaps g)) := by
    rw [← map_sum, hsum, map_smul, LinearMap.trace_one, smul_eq_mul]
  obtain ⟨m, hm⟩ : ∃ m : ℤ, (m : ℂ) = μ * (Module.finrank ℂ E : ℂ) := by
    rw [htr]
    have hmem : ∀ g ∈ S, LinearMap.trace ℂ E ((rightTransl g).restrict (hmaps g)) ∈
        (Int.castRingHom ℂ).range := fun g hg => by
      obtain ⟨k, hk⟩ := hint g hg
      exact ⟨k, by simpa using hk⟩
    obtain ⟨m, hm⟩ := (Int.castRingHom ℂ).range.sum_mem hmem
    exact ⟨m, by simpa using hm⟩
  have hd : (Module.finrank ℂ E : ℂ) ≠ 0 := by
    rw [Nat.cast_ne_zero, Ne, Submodule.finrank_eq_zero]
    exact hμf
  -- So `μ = m / dim E` is rational, and a root of the monic integer polynomial `B.charpoly`.
  have hq : (((m : ℚ) / (Module.finrank ℂ E : ℚ) : ℚ) : ℂ) = μ := by
    push_cast
    rw [hm]
    field_simp
  have hroot : Polynomial.aeval ((m : ℚ) / (Module.finrank ℂ E : ℚ)) B.charpoly = 0 := by
    have h1 : Polynomial.aeval μ B.charpoly = 0 := by
      rw [Matrix.charpoly_map, Polynomial.IsRoot, Polynomial.eval_map] at hμ
      rw [Polynomial.aeval_def, algebraMap_int_eq]
      exact hμ
    rw [← hq, ← eq_ratCast (algebraMap ℚ ℂ), Polynomial.aeval_algebraMap_apply] at h1
    exact (algebraMap ℚ ℂ).injective (by rw [h1, map_zero])
  -- By the rational root theorem, `μ` is an integer.
  obtain ⟨k, hk⟩ := isInteger_of_is_root_of_monic (Matrix.charpoly_monic B) hroot
  refine ⟨k, ?_⟩
  rw [← hq, ← hk]
  simp

/--
**Cayley graphs on normal sets of involutions are integral.** Let $G$ be a finite group and let
$R \subseteq G$ be a set of involutions ($r^2 = 1$ and $r \ne 1$ for every $r \in R$) that is
closed under conjugation ($g r g^{-1} \in R$ for all $r \in R$ and $g \in G$) and generates $G$.
Then the Cayley graph $\mathrm{Cay}(G, R)$, whose vertices are the elements of $G$ with distinct
$x$ and $y$ adjacent when $x^{-1} y \in R$ or $y^{-1} x \in R$, is integral: every eigenvalue of
its integer adjacency matrix (every complex root of the characteristic polynomial of that matrix)
is an integer.

The Kourovka Archive proof: every eigenvalue of $\mathrm{Cay}(G, R)$ has the form
$\frac{1}{\chi(1)} \sum_{r \in R} \chi(r)$ for an irreducible character $\chi$ of $G$; this number
is rational because characters take integer values on involutions, and it is an algebraic integer.

Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 19.50 (D. V.
Lytkina); resolution: (a) D. O. Revin, letter of 21 April 2018; A. Abdollahi, letter of 3 May 2018;
(b) W. Guo, D. V. Lytkina, V. D. Mazurov and D. O. Revin, Integral Cayley graphs, Algebra Logic 58
(2019), no. 4, 297-305, doi:10.1007/s10469-019-09550-2, Kourovka Archive, Problem 19.50: (a) Yes,
with a character-theoretic proof in the Archive (each eigenvalue is (1/χ(1)) ∑_{r ∈ R} χ(r),
rational since characters take integer values on involutions); (b) Yes,
https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.0300v49.
-/
theorem involutionCayleyGraphIntegral_holds : involutionCayleyGraphIntegral := by
  intro G _ _ _ R hinv hconj _
  classical
  convert hasIntegralSpectrum_adjMatrix_mulCayley_of_involutions R hinv hconj

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.IntegralCayleyGraphsKourovka19Problem50Wanted
